### JobKeep — every route, in the order you would actually use them.
#
# Works in Visual Studio, VS Code (REST Client extension) and Rider with no account
# and no external service. Committed on purpose: it is version-controlled next to the
# routes it documents, so it moves in the same commit when a route moves.
#
# Start Postgres and the app first:
#   docker run -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=dev -e POSTGRES_DB=jobkeep postgres:16-alpine
#   cd src && dotnet run

@host = http://localhost:5080

### Create an application. Company and posting are created or reused by name.
# @name create
POST {{host}}/applications
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "company": "Canva",
  "title": "Backend Engineer",
  "location": "Melbourne",
  "description": "Build and operate .NET services on AWS.",
  "sourceUrl": "https://example.com/jobs/123",
  "notes": "Applied via referral"
}

### Capture the id from the response above for everything below.
@applicationId = {{create.response.body.id}}

### List every application, newest first (full eager-loaded graph).
GET {{host}}/applications

### Fetch one.
GET {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}

### Attach a skill. Returns 200, not 201 — and re-sending is a no-op, not an error.
POST {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/skills
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "skillName": "C#",
  "category": "Language",
  "isRequired": true
}

### Attach a requirement. Requirement text is NOT deduplicated.
POST {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/requirements
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "text": "5+ years .NET",
  "kind": "Qualification",
  "isMustHave": true
}

### Update. PATCH semantics: only the fields you send are applied.
PATCH {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "Interviewing",
  "notes": "Phone screen booked for Thursday"
}

### Unlink a skill. The name travels in the path, so encode it: C# is C%23.
# The shared skills row survives — only the join row goes.
DELETE {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/skills/C%23

### Remove a requirement. Scoped to the parent: passing another application's id 404s.
DELETE {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}/requirements/{{requirementId}}

### Delete the application. The posting and company deliberately survive.
DELETE {{host}}/applications/{{applicationId}}

### --- GraphQL, same data layer ---

### Query. Note enums come back SCREAMING_CASE here and PascalCase over REST.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ applications { id status posting { title company { name } postingSkills { isRequired skill { name } } } } }"
}

### Mutation — the Phase 2.1 shape: an input object in, a response DTO out.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation($id: UUID!) { addSkillToPosting(applicationId: $id, input: { skillName: \"Terraform\", category: \"Infra\", isRequired: true }) { skillName category isRequired source } }",
  "variables": { "id": "{{applicationId}}" }
}

### A bad id returns NOT_FOUND here and 404 over REST — the same handler decided both.
POST {{host}}/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation { addSkillToPosting(applicationId: \"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000\", input: { skillName: \"C#\", isRequired: true }) { skillName } }"
}
